.TH PAM_HIRO 8 "August 2026" "HIRO" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
pam_hiro \- PAM module for HIRO face authentication
.SH SYNOPSIS
auth    sufficient    pam_hiro.so [socket=PATH] [timeout_ms=N] [keyring] [debug]
session optional    pam_hiro.so [socket=PATH]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBpam_hiro.so\fR asks the \fBhirod\fR(8) daemon for a face verdict for the
user being authenticated.
.PP
Verdict mapping:
.RS
.PD 0
.TP
face match
PAM_SUCCESS (with \fBsufficient\fR this skips the password)
.TP
face match with \fBkeyring\fR and a released password
the password is injected as \fBPAM_AUTHTK\fR and the module returns
PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL, so the stack continues: \fBpam_unix\fR verifies it
silently and a following keyring module (e.g. \fBpam_gnome_keyring\fR)
unlocks the login keyring without prompting
.TP
no match
PAM_AUTH_ERR (password fallback proceeds)
.TP
daemon unreachable or camera unavailable
PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL (password fallback)
.TP
unexpected failure
PAM_SYSTEM_ERR
.RE
.PD
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B socket=PATH
Daemon socket (default \fI/run/hirod/hirod.sock\fR).
.TP
.B timeout_ms=N
Per-attempt cap in milliseconds (default 5000).
.TP
.B keyring
On a face match, ask \fBhirod\fR for the sealed login password and feed it
to the rest of the stack as \fBPAM_AUTHTK\fR so the login keyring unlocks.
Only meaningful on services whose stack ends with \fBpam_gnome_keyring\fR
or \fBpam_kwallet\fR (graphical greeters, session login). Requires
\fI[keyring] enabled = true\fR and \fBhiro keyring set\fR; the daemon
re-verifies the password against the account before releasing it.
.TP
.B debug
Verbose logging via \fBpam_syslog\fR.
.SH SESSION HOOK
Add \fBsession optional pam_hiro.so\fR to the service's stack (the packaged
pam-auth-update profile does this automatically). When a login completes,
the module's session hook reports it to \fBhirod\fR so the after-reboot
password gate (see \fBsecurity.require_password_after_boot\fR in
\fBhiro.conf\fR(5)) arms face auth for that user until the next reboot.
The hook is best-effort and never blocks or fails the session.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# Debian/Ubuntu: enable via pam-auth-update (adds the keyring option and
# the session hook), or add to the graphical greeter's stack, e.g.
# /etc/pam.d/gdm-password:
auth    sufficient    pam_hiro.so keyring
session optional    pam_hiro.so
# (gdm-password already runs "auth optional pam_gnome_keyring.so" after
#  common-auth, which is what unlocks the keyring with the injected token)
.fi
.SH SECURITY
The module never holds the camera or templates, performs no recognition
itself, and never blocks login: on any failure the password path remains
available.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hiro (1),
.BR hirod (8),
.BR hiro.conf (5)
